\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The operator domain and real-time evolution}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The domain of the Hamiltonian is #{\Dom{H}}. That is the
spelling this Part uses, and #{\extD} is not that domain.
Another printed spelling is #{D(H)}.}

\p{The Hamiltonian of \ref{fdip-007W} is #{H=-\log\widehat T} for
the normalized transfer operator #{\widehat T}; it is nonnegative
and self-adjoint.
It generates the semigroup #{\ue^{-\tau H}} for #{\tau\ge 0}.
Physics writes the unitary group #{\ue^{-\ui tH}}.
The scalar #{\ui} makes the generator #{-\ui H} skew-adjoint
on the complexification, since #{H^{\dagger}=H} gives
#{(-\ui H)^{\dagger}=\ui H}. The time axis is a line with a
positive quadratic form: it has no unit bivector of a plane, and
its pseudoscalar, the unit vector itself, squares to #{+1}.}
